  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Beyond quadratics lie cubics, quartics and higher. Two questions dominate: where are the <strong>zeros</strong>, and what does the graph do at its <strong>ends</strong>? The leading term answers the second on its own.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — End behaviour &amp; the factor theorem",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>The end behaviour of a polynomial is governed entirely by its <em>leading term</em> \\( a x^{n} \\): the sign of \\( a \\) and the parity of \\( n \\). And \\( (x-r) \\) is a factor exactly when \\( r \\) is a zero (\\( f(r)=0 \\)).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Change the degree and the leading coefficient below. Watch how the arms of the graph flip with the sign of \\( a \\), and how an even degree sends both arms the same way while an odd degree sends them opposite ways.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "canvasId": "polyCanvas",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Degree, sign &amp; end behaviour",
          "ar": null
        },
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "A polynomial graph whose degree and leading coefficient can be changed",
          "ar": null
        },
